Building TigerVNC with MSVC
===========================

MinGW is the supported Windows toolchain. This directory exists so that
trying MSVC does not mean changing the normal sources.


The compatibility layer
-----------------------

When CMake detects MSVC it puts contrib/msvc/include on the include path
and force-includes msvc_compat.h (/FI). Between them they supply:

  - the POSIX headers the sources include and MSVC does not ship:
    strings.h, unistd.h, libgen.h, sys/time.h
  - strcasecmp, strncasecmp, ffs, gettimeofday, usleep, dirname, mkdir
  - mode_t, ssize_t, PATH_MAX
  - the GCC attributes the sources are decorated with, which compile away
  - the alternative operator spellings, via <iso646.h>

Nothing under common/, vncviewer/ or tests/ has to know about any of it.


The one thing a header cannot absorb
------------------------------------

win/wm_hooks/wm_hooks.cxx keeps its state in a shared section so that
every process the DLL is injected into sees one copy. That attribute is
not a diagnostic, so compiling it away would leave a DLL that builds and
silently does not work. It is bracketed by data_seg pragmas instead.

Worth knowing on the MinGW side as well: clang honours the section() half
of that attribute and ignores the shared half, so a clang-built DLL has
the section without IMAGE_SCN_MEM_SHARED (0x10000000) set. Only gcc
honours both. Check the section characteristics if it matters to you.


Dependencies
------------

Most come from vcpkg -- zlib, libjpeg-turbo, pixman, nettle, gtest,
gettext, and fltk (the port is 1.3.x, which is the version TigerVNC
wants):

  cmake -S . -B build -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" -A ARM64     -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=<vcpkg>/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake     -DVCPKG_TARGET_TRIPLET=arm64-windows

GnuTLS is not one of them. Its vcpkg port is marked

  "supports": "!windows | mingw"

so a Windows triplet legitimately contains no gnutls.lib, and that
absence is not evidence that the build cannot link. Build it separately
and point CMake at it:

  -DGNUTLS_INCLUDE_DIR=<gnutls>/include
  -DGNUTLS_LIBRARY=<gnutls>/lib/gnutls.lib


Building GnuTLS for MSVC, and for ARM64
---------------------------------------

The ShiftMediaProject ports (github.com/ShiftMediaProject) carry MSVC
solutions for zlib, gmp, nettle and gnutls. They ship Win32 and x64
configurations only, so ARM64 has to be added.

Recorded here because it is the part that costs a day if you have to
work it out again. No library source needs changing -- every edit is to
msbuild project files:

  zlib     SMP/libzlib.sln, SMP/libzlib.vcxproj
  gmp      SMP/libgmp.sln, SMP/libgmp.vcxproj, and the six gen-*.vcxproj
           host tools (gen-bases, gen-fac, gen-fib, gen-jacobitab,
           gen-psqr, gen-trialdivtab)
  nettle   SMP/libnettle.sln, SMP/libnettle.vcxproj,
           SMP/libnettle_deps.sln, SMP/libhogweed.vcxproj,
           SMP/desdata.vcxproj, SMP/eccdata.vcxproj
  gnutls   SMP/libgnutls.sln, SMP/libgnutls.vcxproj,
           SMP/libgnutls_deps.sln

In each: add Debug|ARM64 and Release|ARM64 next to the existing Win32 and
x64 entries -- plus the DLL, WinRT and DLLStaticDeps variants where the
solution has them -- each with a matching PlatformToolset property group.

One more, outside those repositories: ShiftMediaProject/VSYASM. Its
yasm.props hard-fails on any platform that is not Win32 or x64, and its
install_script.bat does not recognise an ARM64 host. Both need an ARM64
case adding.

Build in dependency order -- zlib, gmp, nettle, gnutls -- then collect
the headers, import libraries and DLLs into one tree to point at.


Known limitations
-----------------

  - The viewer does not build yet. vncviewer.cxx puts a preprocessor
    directive inside a macro argument list, which is undefined behaviour
    that gcc accepts and MSVC rejects.
  - ENABLE_NLS=OFF does not build, and not only with MSVC.
